</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-11-29.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="065803"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#065803" label="[06:58:03]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: sounds like it has very a specific customer base that probably is locked into it and can't migrate</span></div><div class="line" id="142730"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#142730" label="[14:27:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pabs3: For something as expensive as nonstop servers (which are $100K+ per), the customers are not people but companies. Companies don't get locked into Operating Systems, they get locked into userspace programs that produce value. So either the companies that &quot;support&quot; those applications are dead (and then they really should be planning a migration away) or are unwilling to port their code to Linux (usually can be changed with an offer</span></div><div class="line" id="142730"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#142730" label="[14:27:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">of money but usually a good sign of them being unable to actually understand their application enough to port)</span></div><br /></div></body></html>